{:edb/db-before-t 1900 :edb/db-after-t 1901 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535213 50 #inst "2026-10-08T19:42:21.672Z" 13194139535213 true] [17592186091871 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/c86e5a3dd066efc462635310ff80973f468d04bad9206f410a74941ccf39f2f3.png)\n\nFind $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = 2$  (shown above) at $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$" 13194139535213 true] [17592186091871 1100 "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = 2$ (shown above) at $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/998/Source/Images/b6c3d21c49c399c4407247101d36d938.png)" 13194139535213 false] [17592186091871 1145 "To find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwe use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r = 2$, we get\n\n$$\nx = 2\\cos θ, y = 2\\sin θ\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{′}(θ) &= -2\\sin θ, y^{′}(θ) = 2\\cos θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2\\cos θ}{-2\\sin θ}; =-\\cot θ\n$$\n\nFinally, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} \\mid _{θ = π/3} &= -\\cot (\\frac{π}{3}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 false] [17592186091871 1145 "To find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r = 2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /3} & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 true] [17592186092035 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/19b420204caf7f2706b4d355b5538dc347b5377d2b9f99297dbf6accc3220f59.png)\n\nFind $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = \\theta,$ shown above." 13194139535213 true] [17592186092035 1100 "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = \\theta$, shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/998/Source/Images/11712254a9347c799840b9c88b9a8ee1.png)" 13194139535213 false] [17592186092035 1145 "To find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwe use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\theta$, we get\n\n$$\nx = \\theta\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=\\theta\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta)$:\n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta$ using the product rule:\n$x^{′}(θ)|= (θ\\cos θ)^{′}; = (θ)^{′} \\cdot \\cos θ + θ \\cdot (\\cos θ)^{′}; = 1 \\cdot \\cos θ + θ \\cdot (-\\sin θ); = \\cos θ - θ\\sin θ$\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta$ using the product rule:\n$y^{′}(θ)|= (θ\\sin θ)^{′}; = (θ)^{′} \\cdot \\sin θ + θ \\cdot (\\sin θ)^{′}; = 1 \\cdot \\sin θ + θ \\cdot \\cos θ; = \\sin θ + θ\\cos θ$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (y^{′}(θ))/(x^{′}(θ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sin θ + θ\\cos θ}{\\cos θ - θ\\sin θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 false] [17592186092035 1145 "To find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\nx = \\theta\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=\\theta\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta$ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =(\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta$ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =(\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 true] [17592186092104 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/a0eb20697f72b1ec5f06cc1ebfa4ba364c1023c915950b64cfab2367964db928.png)\n\nFind $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = 2^{\\theta}$ (shown above) given that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta) = 2^{\\theta}(\\ln 2\\sin\\theta+ \\cos\\theta), \\qquad\nx'(\\theta) = 2^{\\theta}(\\ln 2\\cos\\theta-\\sin\\theta) .\n$$" 13194139535213 true] [17592186092104 1100 "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = 2^{\\theta}$ (shown above) given that\n$y'(\\theta) = 2^{\\theta}(\\ln 2\\sin\\theta+ \\cos\\theta), \\qquad x'(\\theta) = 2^{\\theta}(\\ln 2\\cos\\theta-\\sin\\theta)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/998/Source/Images/e2076809a74372e836b4592936b7585b.png)" 13194139535213 false] [17592186092104 1145 "To find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwe use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (2^{θ}(\\ln 2\\sin θ + \\cos θ))/(2^{θ}(\\ln 2\\cos θ - \\sin θ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\ln 2\\sin θ + \\cos θ}{\\ln 2\\cos θ - \\sin θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 false] [17592186092104 1145 "To find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{2}^{\\theta }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{{2}^{\\theta }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 true] [17592186092105 1100 "Find the slope of the tangent line to the polar curve $r = 1-\\cos\\theta$ at $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$, given that\n$y'(\\theta) = \\cos\\theta - \\cos2\\theta, \\qquad x'(\\theta) = -\\sin\\theta + \\sin2\\theta$." 13194139535213 false] [17592186092105 1100 "Find the slope of the tangent line to the polar curve $r = 1-\\cos\\theta$ at $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ given that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta) = \\cos\\theta  - \\cos2\\theta, \\qquad\nx'(\\theta) = -\\sin\\theta + \\sin2\\theta.\n$$" 13194139535213 true] [17592186092105 1145 "To find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwe use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the numerator and denominator at\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac\\pi4\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}) &= \\cos (\\frac{π}{4}) - \\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}) &= -\\sin (\\frac{π}{4}) + \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} \\mid _{θ = π/4} &= (y^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}))/(x^{′}(\\frac{π}{4})) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}))/((-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} \\mid _{θ = π/4} &= ((\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}))/((-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + 1)) \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}))/(2 \\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + 1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{-\\sqrt{2} + 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2 - \\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2 - \\sqrt{2}} \\cdot \\frac{2 + \\sqrt{2}}{2 + \\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2\\sqrt{2} + 2}{2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 false] [17592186092105 1145 "To find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the numerator and denominator at $\\theta=\\dfrac\\pi4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{-\\sqrt{2}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2-\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2-\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{2+\\sqrt{2}}{2+\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+2}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 true] [17592186092183 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337899 13194139535213 true] [17592186092183 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337947 13194139535213 true] [17592186092183 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337950 13194139535213 true] [17592186092183 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337954 13194139