\n$$\nx={e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y={e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={({e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={({e}^{-3\\theta })}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3\\theta }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={({e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={({e}^{-3\\theta })}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\theta }(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-3\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{-{e}^{-3\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 true] [17592186337900 1000 #edb/ref 1759218