peratorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[(\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{(\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[(\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}{\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535213 true] [17592186337948 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337949 13194139535213 true] [